now many young people here in germany  say they're disappointed with the  results of the election they say they  feel ignored  if it had been up to first time in young  voters to decide on the new government  the greens would have ended up as the  strongest party the market friendly fdp  would have taken second place with 21  the ultimate winner of the election the  spd would only have received 15  of the vote and the conservative blocked  the cdu  a mere 10 that's a result of polling of  course the left party and the left-wing  afd received the smallest percentage of  the youth vote now dw reporter axel  robot went to meet some young voters in  the eastern german city of leipzig to  get a sense of what they think about  their nation's political leaders  it's the end of term  18 year old oscar sets his high school  diploma next year  like nearly 3 million other first-time  voters he was able to take part in  germany's elections  oscar is interested in politics  he's not a party member but he's active  in his local youth parliament  he's hoping for a centrist  conservative-led government  in coalition with the liberal fdp  and the greens  i found the election a bit dull  i hope that we get a stable government  not like in italy where there's a new  leader every few months we need  stability  oscar's friend tanya also voted for the  first time  i'm tanya vana i thought the election  was shocking and surprising  i'd like a filter coffee to take away  and i'll have a caller  tanya was shocked that the far-right  alternative for germany emerged as the  strongest party in two of the 16 federal  states including here in saxony she  wanted to see a left-led alliance of the  spd greens and the left party but they  didn't get enough votes  it's not at all clear what kind of  coalition government we're going to get  it's doubtful whether it's going to be  as progressive as we are hoping  and that's a bit disheartening  i'm not so worried about the actual  lineup with the strong fdp and greens  and a relatively weak cdu and spd  it should mean the policies of the  smaller coalition partners move up the  agenda  of course there are going to be huge  discussions with either the cdu or the  sbd on which policies make it into the  coalition agreement  and where there will have to be  compromises  whatever happens i think it's going to  be a really exciting government  if only young voters like tanya and  oscar had taken part in the election the  result would have been very different  big gains for the greens and liberals  with the spd and conservatives sidelined  the environment comes first and we need  a proper plan not just talk okay there's  a target to stop burning coal by 2030.  that's true but you also need a plan for  what to replace it with  a big question for me is what pensions  will be like in 30 to 40 years time  and how that will work  that was an important factor in how i  voted  it's all changed after 16 years of  merkel  first we've got to get through the next  four years without her  and that could be tricky  these young voters have only known  germany under angela merkel  when she started as chancellor they were  just  well joining us for more now is tobias  bachala he's a freshly elected member of  the green party in the german parliament  uh mr bakla thanks for being with us  this morning uh you like many people who  voted for your party in the market  friendly fdp you're under 30. so first  of all tell us what do you think this  election result means for young people  including first-time voters in germany  yeah good morning mr martin i think it's  an interesting result because it shows  that for a young generation more than  ever and more than regarding with the  whole population  they asked for a whole new approach  within politics i believe that the idea  of simply managing crisis when they  already appeared  is something that we've seen within the  last years and i believe that's  something which needs to end um of  course as a green i'm talking about like  the climate crisis is something we can't  manage when it already is happening we  need  to prepare for it before it's actually  there but i think in germany we've seen  that with many little things with many  little crisis uh where the big coalition  of the two old big parties um yeah was  mainly stuck with waiting until a  problem actually occurred um instead of  yeah trying to prevent crisis and um i  think that the young generation is  wishing more than others for that to end  when you're out campaigning for this  election talking to people  what did you hear what did young people  in particularly tell you were their main  concerns  i mean digitalization is a big topic in  germany and for the young people i mean  we have been within the corona crisis  struggling with digital policies but  mainly um like with digital  infrastructure in the schools and i  believe that changed the view for many  young people and showed us that we are  actually quite behind when it comes to  digital infrastructure and  this is a big big topic and it occurred  within the corona crisis in nutshell  but of course also the climate crisis i  mean we have people striking on friday  and it has been over half a million  people and again once again a lot of  young people have been there um so i  think those were two big issues pushing  forward and i think that also shows in  the result with strong greens and strong  liberals who  set um digitization on the agenda i mean  we did that as well but um the liberals  pushed it probably it within the  campaign a bit more so i think  this is uh something that shows a lot  to  a freshly elected member of the german  parliament for the green party thank you  very much for talking with us  thanks a lot
 

